able, so they may not increase heart attack risk as much as soft plaques from a sedentary lifestyle. - **Atrial fibrillation** – A well‑established finding: male endurance athletes have a 2–5 times higher lifetime risk of atrial fibrillation (irregular heartbeat). This is believed to stem from chronic stretching and scarring of the atrial tissue from high volumes over many years. - **Myocardial fibrosis** – Some MRI studies show “patchy fibrosis” (tiny scars) in the heart muscle of older athletes who have trained intensely for decades. This is not the same as heart failure, but it can reduce the heart’s relaxation ability and increase arrhythmia risk. Importantly, these changes are **not the same as heart deterioration from a sedentary lifestyle** – they are different types of wear. A sedentary person’s heart deteriorates from stiffening, clogged arteries, and weak muscle. An athlete’s heart may develop subtle scarring and electrical changes but typically remains very functional. ### 4. The role of genetics and individual vulnerability Many elite athletes live into old age with perfectly healthy hearts. Those who show signs of wear often have an underlying predisposition (e.g., a gene for arrhythmia, high blood pressure, or inflammation). The extreme training simply “uncovers” that vulnerability, rather than causing damage in a completely healthy person. ### Bottom line For the vast majority of people, regular aerobic exercise **strengthens the heart and prolongs life**. The “wear and tear” seen in some lifelong elite athletes is a rare, dose‑dependent phenomenon that becomes noticeable only after decades of training at levels far beyond what most people do. It is not a reason to avoid exercise – it’s a reminder that even good things can have subtle trade‑offs at extreme doses, and that balance and recovery remain important even for the fittest.
