ng that a 75‑year‑old lifelong runner has “some stiffening,” that stiffening is **less** than that of a sedentary 75‑year‑old. It is not caused by running; it is caused by living for 75 years. Running merely slowed the rate of change. ### Key studies to consider - **Masters Athletes Study** (Möhlenkamp et al., 2008): showed that male endurance athletes had more coronary calcium, but the plaques were denser (more stable) and they lived longer. - **HEART Study** (Baggish et al., 2010): older runners had larger hearts but **normal diastolic function** – no pathological stiffening. - **Cardiac MRI in older athletes** (Wilson et al., 2011): very low prevalence of myocardial fibrosis (<10%) only in those with extreme lifetime training volumes (>1000 hours of vigorous activity). Most recreational runners had none. ### The logical conclusion If moderate lifelong exercise caused harmful deterioration, we would expect lifelong runners to have **higher** rates of heart failure, arrhythmias, and early death. But the opposite is true: they have dramatically lower rates of all cardiovascular events and live longer. The small, benign changes that are sometimes detectable on sensitive imaging are part of normal aging, not damage from exercise. So no, moderate use does not cause deterioration – it **protects** against the deterioration that would otherwise occur from aging and inactivity. The “wear and tear” you’re worried about is just aging itself, and exercise is one of the best ways to slow it down.
